Scope per manifest: web/templates/pages/major-expenses.html (two new <label> blocks) +
internal/handlers/majorexpenses/handlers.go (one struct-literal field added to
parseExpenseForm) + handlers_test.go (5 new tests). Confirmed via
`git diff HEAD -- <file>` hunk headers on both source files — no stray edits, no touch to
form-level hx-trigger/hx-target/hx-swap attributes, no touch to HC/SY1/SY3 territory.

ATTACK 1 — Classic checkbox-unset bug on the UPDATE path (unchecking posts nothing; does
"absent" correctly mean false, or does update silently keep a stale true?). Read
parseFormBool (handlers.go:540-544): "absent key -> false" by construction, doc-commented.
Read UpdateMajorExpense (dataloader/major_expenses.go:110-138, SY1-authored): line 126
unconditionally assigns `list[i].ExcludeFromPlanSync = updates.ExcludeFromPlanSync` on every
update call — no OR-in, no "only if true" guard, no merge with the prior stored value. Since
handleUpdate always calls parseExpenseForm fresh from the CURRENT POST body (never merges with
the existing record field-by-field for this field), an update that omits the checkbox
computes false and overwrites true with false. Independently verified (not just trusting the
test name) by reading TestHandleUpdate_ExcludeFromPlanSync_Persists in full: it posts the flag
on, asserts persisted true, then re-PUTs the SAME id WITHOUT the checkbox and asserts
persisted false — this is exactly the two-step check-then-uncheck sequence the classic bug
requires; it is not a single-direction test. Not a defect.

ATTACK 2 — Edit-form checked-state isolation across N expenses (id/name collisions). Read the
template's row structure: `major-expense-item` is a named template invoked per-`.Summaries`
row, with `data-expense-id`, `id="major-expense-item-{{.Expense.ID}}"`, and
`id="major-expense-detail-{{.Expense.ID}}"` all keyed on the per-row Expense.ID — Go template
range scoping gives each invocation its own `.Expense`, so `{{if .Expense.ExcludeFromPlanSync}}`
cannot leak a sibling row's state by construction (no shared/global template variable holds
it). Cross-checked against TestMajorExpenseEditForm_RendersExcludeFromPlanSyncCheckedState,
which seeds one flagged + one unflagged expense and slices the response body on the two
distinct `major-expense-detail-<id>` anchors — confirmed the test actually asserts on
isolated substrings, not the whole body. One test-QUALITY (not product) weakness noted: the
flagged-side assertion is `strings.Contains(flaggedSection, "checked")` with NO windowing
around the specific input (unlike the unflagged-side assertion, which correctly windows
`idx:idx+200`), so it would not, by itself, prove "checked" landed on the correct input if some
other control in that ~2-3KB fragment also produced the literal substring "checked". This does
NOT let the fixture pass vacuously here (grepped the full edit-form fragment: no other control
renders "checked" when IsInternalTransfer is false, which it is in this fixture), and I
independently confirmed the ACTUAL app markup is correct by direct read
(major-expenses.html:1120-1123: `{{if .Expense.ExcludeFromPlanSync}}checked{{end}}` sits
directly inside the `name="exclude_from_plan_sync"` input, immediately after the sibling
is_internal_transfer block) — so this is a backlog test-hardening observation, not a FAIL
basis.

ATTACK 3 — Does the create form reset properly after submit (leftover checked state
persisting into the next "Add")? Read the form's `hx-on::after-request` handler
(major-expenses.html:1175): on success it calls `this.reset()`, which resets EVERY field
(including the new checkbox) to the form's markup-declared initial state. The create form's
exclude_from_plan_sync input has no `checked` attribute in its static markup (line 1232-1233,
no `{{if}}` — correctly, a brand-new expense has no prior state to reflect), so reset always
returns it to unchecked. Not a defect.

ATTACK 4 — hx-include name collision. The edit form (and the create form) both carry
`hx-include="#major-expenses-filter-form"`, which merges an ENTIRELY SEPARATE form's fields
into the PUT/POST body — if that filter form happened to also have a field literally named
`exclude_from_plan_sync` or `is_internal_transfer`, Go's `r.FormValue` (first-value-wins)
could silently ignore the edit form's own checkbox state. Grepped the filter form's fields:
only `start` and `end` (date range) — no collision. Pre-existing pattern anyway (SY2 didn't
touch hx-include), so even a genuine collision would be out of SY2's scope. Not a defect.

ATTACK 5 — Cross-check with SY1: does a def flagged via THIS UI actually flow into
computeDashboardSync's exclusions? Traced statically: handleAdd appends the whole parsed
struct via AddMajorExpense (dataloader/major_expenses.go:86-104, no field-by-field copy to
drop it), UpdateMajorExpense explicitly copies it (line 126), and SY1's computeDashboardSync
loads via the SAME `loader.LoadMajorExpenses()` call. Then PROVED it live: threw a probe test
(added/run/deleted, internal/handlers/majorexpenses/zz_probe_sy2check_sy1integration_test.go,
deletion confirmed via `git status --porcelain`) that POSTs through the real `handleAdd`
handler with `exclude_from_plan_sync=on` and a keyword matching the test fixture's "My
Landlord LLC" transactions, then calls SY1's real, unmodified
`majorexpenses.ComputePlanSyncExclusions` against the loader's real on-disk state:
    exclusions map size=2, matched landlord transactions=2
Both landlord outflows the UI-created def should exclude were present in SY1's exclusion map —
the full UI-to-classifier chain is genuinely wired, not merely independently round-tripping on
each side.

ATTACK 6 — ACCESSIBILITY.md point 10 (focus restoration after an HTMX swap). The edit form's
swap-on-change wiring (`hx-trigger="change delay:500ms"`, `hx-target="#major-expenses-results"`,
`hx-swap="innerHTML"`) is PRE-EXISTING and shared verbatim by every other field in that form
(name, keywords, min/max, notes, is_internal_transfer) — confirmed via the git diff hunk
headers that SY2 added only the two `<label>` blocks and touched no form-level hx-* attribute.
Any focus-restoration gap in this pattern is identical for the new checkbox and its siblings,
predates SY2, and is out of SY2's written scope (observation for the backlog if not already
tracked, not a FAIL basis here).

